## Bio

Bill Llia began his work not in a boardroom, but on the ground — walking the wards and villages of Nairobi County, listening to families struggling to access basic healthcare and young people searching for a path forward. Over 12 years, that work has shaped every decision he makes.

Starting as a community health volunteer, Bill helped establish four health centers in underserved wards — clinics that today serve thousands of families who previously traveled hours for care. He has led skills training and mentorship programs that equipped over 500 young people with market-ready skills. His understanding of Nairobi County comes from being in the communities, not reading reports about them.

Bill is running for Governor because he has seen what works. He has seen a health center transform a neighborhood. He has seen a young person with the right training launch a business that employs others. These are not theories — they are his life's work. He is not a career politician asking for a promotion. He is a community servant asking for a chance to serve at scale.

## Journey

In 2012, Bill laced up his boots and hit the ground as a **Community Health Volunteer** — going door-to-door in Nairobi's underserved wards, connecting families to care they had been denied for years.

By 2016, he became a **Health Program Coordinator**, overseeing the establishment of two community health centers in wards that had gone decades without proper facilities — centers that now see hundreds of patients each month.

In 2020, as **Youth Empowerment Lead**, Bill launched skills training programs that put over 500 young Nairobi County residents on a path to employment and entrepreneurship.

In 2024, Bill answered the call to run for **Governor of Nairobi County** — not as a politician with a script, but as a servant leader with a record and a plan.

## Why Running

I am running because I have stood beside mothers who lost their children to preventable diseases. I have sat with young people who have degrees but no jobs. I have watched small business owners shut their doors because the system failed them. These are not statistics. They are my neighbors.

These problems are solvable. I have proven it — four health centers standing where there were none, 500 young people building futures instead of waiting for handouts. With focused leadership and transparent governance, we can deliver this across every ward in Nairobi County. I have the experience. I have the plan. And I have the will to see it through.

I am not asking for your vote. I am asking for your trust — and I will earn it the same way I always have: by showing up, listening hard, and delivering results that change lives.
